#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "2ce03c34-8e2c-5554-a129-1eaf05ad46fd" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-12548" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(3,2)$ and the line $x=5$." :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/526/Source/Images/075ce483c7effa23b544c4e110e41b82.png)\n\nSince the line $x = 5$ is vertical, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(3,2)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $x$-coordinate of the point and $5$.\n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd = \\mid 3 - 5 \\mid = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "64a6782b-aa64-5027-8311-3498c19c39e1" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-12549" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$." :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/526/Source/Images/20c50cfc96d069b4dd030c77c6959b60.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x - 5\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(1,3)$.\n\nThe given line has a slope of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nso our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-2$.\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 3 &= -2(x - 1) \\\\\ny - 3 &= -2x + 2 \\\\\ny &= -2x + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = \\frac{1}{2}x - 5; y =-2x + 5\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by setting the two equations equal and solving for $x$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}x - 5 &= -2x + 5 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}x + 2x &= 5 + 5 \\\\\n\\frac{5}{2}x &= 10 \\\\\nx &= 10 \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3$. So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3$. Consequently, the lines intersect at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(4,-3\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,3)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(4, -3 \\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\sqrt{(x_{2} - x_{1})^{2} + (y_{2} - y_{1})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(4 - 1)^{2} + (-3 - 3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 36} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{45} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9}\\sqrt{5} \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5\n$$\n\nis $3 \\sqrt{5}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073376 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "57fc631b-c251-55d2-a9d5-6b41fc4bb00c" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-12612" :question/problem "Find the point on the line $y-x=4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/526/Source/Images/8d76b170141a85d09dbefe8efdfd59dd.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - x &= 4 \\\\\ny &= x + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to\n\n$$\ny = x + 4\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(3,3)$.\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1$, so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1$.\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 3 &= -1(x - 3) \\\\\ny - 3 &= -x + 3 \\\\\ny &= -x + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{y = x + 4; y =-x + 6\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 4 &= -x + 6 \\\\\nx + x &= 6 - 4 \\\\\n2x &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5$. So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=5$. Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $(1,5)$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y-x= 4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3)$ is $(1,5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a60c3d-1754-566b-8910-cffc9ce6f802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247408} {:answer/id #uuid "204d018e-c16b-530e-bf7a-b75c329fc52d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247409} {:answer/id #uuid "d784237f-8ede-5b02-88fc-86614228987d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247410} {:answer/id #uuid "86ba8fd1-d20d-5cf0-b2d4-7a170b627a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247411} {:answer/id #uuid "8fce9667-6b42-5bc2-8e5a-dddedd0cb878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d784237f-8ede-5b02-88fc-86614228987d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247410} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce57ccc5-4582-5aa3-b24d-c0f2fe9b4412" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f162095a-e7b4-52b0-b16f-7a98cc60e6ca" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-114323" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(4,3)$ and the line $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/ea64c0752738b0c917d897060351cdd43ed7bbf687cc20781b7241ee3be0ad3c.png)\n\nSince the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(4,3)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between the $x$-coordinate of the point and $2.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|4-2|=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f5f015-9087-5c4e-b535-bd49c4b2f589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{90}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247415} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ade176-cbf4-587c-a5d8-17992475a05c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247416} {:answer/id #uuid "c78e19f3-70d9-56c1-a6ea-50b787d3887a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},-\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247417} {:answer/id #uuid "479c1d82-775d-5218-a0f2-3f2d9dfb1035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247418} {:answer/id #uuid "e8902a36-ed80-5987-9b7a-267319183572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{66}{13},-\\frac{18}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "479c1d82-775d-5218-a0f2-3f2d9dfb1035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247418} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfe32756-a5c1-5bb5-a38a-24fb672f9c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247414}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49e0ab8c-4ebb-5b2d-8e5e-32400720dc1f" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-114336" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/69e06e1b9b72e3a4c1fc2e7f2fcd85aa4aa8b34e4fa2f4f5aebb8d1b10474415.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(-3,4).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2+4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =6-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{18}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4)$ is $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b83b79a-3d27-563d-817a-17fce0796043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247422} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ee4382-b711-529c-bafa-5337cfc35ccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247423} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff7d472-b785-5d11-9101-4d8e4f862c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247424} {:answer/id #uuid "b3301e55-6155-51ca-a280-86552f2bdaa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247425} {:answer/id #uuid "431e99be-c9e2-5789-b4b7-9db2caaab681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3ee4382-b711-529c-bafa-5337cfc35ccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247423} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c2e812-cb6a-5580-bf28-e45b01817124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247421}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e79577df-8880-5651-be09-d6a38cdd29b6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-114341" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,-5)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/3a4bbfd80480fe3fcc778a490514d27d65d83abf77ca1752e4203d9b3185b618.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(1,-5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+5 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{17}{3}-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{26}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(4,-3).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,-5)$ and $(4,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-1)^{2}+(-3+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,-5)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36bebfca-66e3-536e-b2d7-230ae61652b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247429} {:answer/id #uuid "16fd2dc9-2117-5499-971e-3acc531cba0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247430} {:answer/id #uuid "29cba391-82ec-586c-adbb-471250c9da41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247431} {:answer/id #uuid "1a58071a-a50b-5aa6-a200-2e2ac43481a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247432} {:answer/id #uuid "ea60e65c-7c57-52c4-8046-97d28472df67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36bebfca-66e3-536e-b2d7-230ae61652b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247429} :answer-field/id #uuid "e728ae88-bf1b-58c8-968b-04a4a468a486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247428}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8a6f517-e603-56a5-8d4e-8cbb84ce1587" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-114343" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/021b08ba0d14e40939103d6d3e9e2004d49e5d047a5f89677b3fe542aa866f82.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x-2$ and passes through the point $(-1,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-(x+1) \\\\ y & =-x-1+5 \\\\ y & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-2 \\\\ y=-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ x+x & =4+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=1.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=1.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,5)$ and $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3+1)^{2}+(1-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2$ is $4\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f91581f5-1f72-5dae-88c1-f6bcfb29be2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247436} {:answer/id #uuid "416bff52-2ca3-5809-9dd3-06101b21863e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247437} {:answer/id #uuid "400691fb-900d-5318-9bfd-096e5742224a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{13},\\frac{3}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247438} {:answer/id #uuid "58f5b96f-8e0f-5d43-be42-2be5f5478d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247439} {:answer/id #uuid "d518c73f-060d-5a2e-a88b-ccd0767ccf2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},-\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58f5b96f-8e0f-5d43-be42-2be5f5478d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247439} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6958058-789f-5cc4-aa89-27ad2f2cdceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247435}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f083c6a-63f4-551e-9ac4-c8f0d3effd8c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-114345" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/ff9810290868f7fd465bc310c969255b92f646d6b59ad71a93a3dcc9c38ef49c.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+2x & =3 \\\\ 3y & =-2x+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5)$ is  $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22435a36-a01f-5169-853b-df19b9d7fd4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247443} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd5714e-c909-5212-813a-93836ab3a06f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247444} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3a4a11-d48d-5cfe-aa22-5c2868265ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247445} {:answer/id #uuid "382fb6a8-0da8-565d-8eb4-d71e3bfc3d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247446} {:answer/id #uuid "417e390a-d43f-5f4f-9275-75c5dbd08654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22435a36-a01f-5169-853b-df19b9d7fd4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247443} :answer-field/id #uuid "86a13e16-d3cc-55f3-9310-c97d9b334a7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247442}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b9d5b1c1-cc35-5675-83c0-9258962522cd" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-114358" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-5,3)$ and the line $2y+5x=10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+5x & =10 \\\\ 2y & =-5x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/8c4238a00b775d4db828651ae2691596bfadafcdc748494cc957bad1cb1b6b91.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5$ and passes through the point $(-5,3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+5) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{2}{5}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{29}{10}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=5.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(0,5).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-5,3)$ and $(0,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0-(-5))}^{2}+(5-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-5,3)$ and the line $2y+5x=10$ is $\\sqrt{29}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0ba03f5-5011-5c62-abe1-b762cb17cff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247450} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9f84bd-11d4-5ee3-8592-2e261036f7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247451} {:answer/id #uuid "11aa50df-2313-5319-bb0f-e046cca3d043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247452} {:answer/id #uuid "79a76960-b7f7-581e-a8ac-8e282e88b1ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247453} {:answer/id #uuid "79b8872a-1686-5334-bff8-879d30f80ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79a76960-b7f7-581e-a8ac-8e282e88b1ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247453} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e3ceee1-bacb-5d42-91fb-82c9f4a68f0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247449}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "93104da6-8eb0-5ad5-8b24-6e007223800c" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-114364" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and the line $2y+3x=-6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+3x & =-6 \\\\ 2y & =-3x-6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/715e1292edaf78979f6b9ae36586a8196dcc30e865915e7eefba2360281f85ec.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-3$ and passes through the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{5}{6}+3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1-2)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and the line $2y+3x=-6$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb14a9b8-35bd-5a1c-9812-1f4cf955d28a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073239} {:answer/id #uuid "c17f492b-7afb-50ef-8a00-e263933db541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073240} {:answer/id #uuid "f096e959-0c07-5c05-9065-fcacdef3c5fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073241} {:answer/id #uuid "029c4470-7187-5688-9ee5-b5e57a676e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073242} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3eceb3-8cd6-566f-847c-b1cc5490d072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "029c4470-7187-5688-9ee5-b5e57a676e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073242} :answer-field/id #uuid "9889c593-708f-5999-b96a-7b15dd26891c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073238}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "119ea192-657f-5438-a868-40ca8f32ea19" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-74034" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(5,-1)$ and the line $y = 2$." :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/f907ee8b0bb92792cc69905678210af71088af6731e5a586eaeed0bfc7856b9c.png)\n\nSince the line $y=2$ is a horizontal line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(5,-1)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $y$-coordinate of the point and $2.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|-1-2|=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8f7c7d4-e70c-5d4d-baea-be96141ede63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073257} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce16184-f1a2-5896-8704-c49d339e0b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, \\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073258} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0133ec-cc00-5cf7-a3a8-b1516648c07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073259} {:answer/id #uuid "67e8903b-94f3-50fa-babc-f0a1bb48e7f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073260} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7314e9-9ff0-5c31-8ec2-c7fa22c053ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b0133ec-cc00-5cf7-a3a8-b1516648c07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073259} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc7b8bce-af09-5f2d-8ae9-c03b3a21bdbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073256}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2a5b92a-6d2c-561b-999b-9dedaf72311f" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-74060" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/526/Source/Images/q-74060.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $2y - 3x = 3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=0.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$ is  $(-1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2bfd806-d96d-56ef-8a17-050382fa91aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073263} {:answer/id #uuid "fede7757-e7a0-5856-9c97-41f31a5a1d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{9}{2}, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073264} {:answer/id #uuid "03a06aec-2288-5d72-b3a4-d001f55825e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073265} {:answer/id #uuid "07ad4d9b-7194-5406-b69d-0c0180c2b050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073266} {:answer/id #uuid "229299ff-0b09-522d-a797-aba1addf9b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "229299ff-0b09-522d-a797-aba1addf9b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073267} :answer-field/id #uuid "e56869ec-9da3-5d01-a62d-b1a42d38a26c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073262}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "853cfa3c-da08-562e-b7e9-24b69cddbfcd" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-74061" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/526/Source/Images/q-74061.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y =-\\frac{2}{3}x - 2$ that is closest to the point $(2, 1)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,-2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1)$ is $(0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "232b6746-6d99-5de2-b1b9-5a25afc28cdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247457} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9fd463-2d41-5b93-9a27-d23edcbbf4ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247458} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c01bbc-ec75-5692-b96d-2afd69220b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247459} {:answer/id #uuid "63d0a784-836a-57c6-a646-e111b0d9485d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247460} {:answer/id #uuid "e41c7aa2-119b-55cd-b769-be3a515c22e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e41c7aa2-119b-55cd-b769-be3a515c22e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247461} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c644a72-fc30-5aee-b7ab-6ee59858c025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247456}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "05beb7e7-037d-5be1-bce1-a95e6e9ca4f6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-74202" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-4)$ and the line $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/d46a6ed76af5c226e327ba197fe019df0d9085e095ca4781898aba54268a1706.png)\n\nSince the line $x=4$ is a vertical line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(-1,-4)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $x$-coordinate of the point and $4.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|-1-4|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e46792ea-7075-5aca-bf20-e3a6d9c93ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247464} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f6e785-bffd-53b0-ba79-56e03699ca7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247465} {:answer/id #uuid "a34949a8-8d02-56ff-bcf7-d369e68644fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247466} {:answer/id #uuid "3ccf102a-7163-5255-950a-9b473eb83575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247467} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf253df-5e36-5bef-9fd6-767ae29de64d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e46792ea-7075-5aca-bf20-e3a6d9c93ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247464} :answer-field/id #uuid "3050d997-e3a0-5f7e-ae55-d00fe2325e09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247463}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa2317dd-ccab-5589-b882-514074d63c86" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-74206" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(2,-3)$ and the line $y=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/2a3107d0db934d4dc9552444badc52914f92d46d7fd83a3e6048d06f3465dcf9.png)\n\nSince the line $y=5$ is a horizontal line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(2,-3)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $y$-coordinate of the point and $5.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|-3-5|=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eeda361-516f-5e20-b0ee-bcc1899b8d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247471} {:answer/id #uuid "f72e879f-83c5-5a60-98a7-32bb58d273de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247472} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc02a8e-9ed1-55c7-b355-a7f1c7062846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247473} {:answer/id #uuid "cfaa9c2d-deda-568a-8698-0c07e949813f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247474} {:answer/id #uuid "e93e5e44-de73-5bd5-89b9-078bfc554789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e93e5e44-de73-5bd5-89b9-078bfc554789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247475} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffb445fa-62c0-5a88-800f-bbcc17615a6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247470}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0d238fa-1e0e-5487-8673-870efc26cffa" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-74207" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-3,3)$ and the line $y=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/67f63247ff1f91fae17f257512849ac2415a3894d57475d1e8a7afee1094df31.png)\n\nSince the line $y=-2$ is a horizontal line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(-3,3)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $y$-coordinate of the point and $-2.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|3-(-2)|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6255463-5820-582f-b710-6cb9ce7f0d9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247478} {:answer/id #uuid "fc889719-669e-5057-a251-e51ea4da88f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247479} {:answer/id #uuid "f21c6d77-c735-5067-b312-7a5d2c649ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247480} {:answer/id #uuid "7fc122d5-1d1e-5697-a372-a904ded26a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247481} {:answer/id #uuid "18bd2741-5630-52c0-b65b-c084ea181bc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18bd2741-5630-52c0-b65b-c084ea181bc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247482} :answer-field/id #uuid "a95cdb93-c8e3-5a95-a41d-02d781042020" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247477}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a823c10-5ebd-503e-9bab-03ff8b0369b1" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-74417" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/eb1176b4a5e2565fceb03e9af652a6ae875f5ee678c103405045fcebc36342e1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x+2$ and passes through the point $(-1,-3).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+3 & =1(x+1) \\\\ y+3 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+2 \\\\ y=x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2 & =x-2 \\\\ -x-x & =-2-2 \\\\ -2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,-3)$ and $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-1))^{2}+(0-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2$ is $3\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cdbc744-f046-5a85-b525-147f31e4c7e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247485} {:answer/id #uuid "125921a3-35e7-53c8-90b8-d8a79d2b91eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247486} {:answer/id #uuid "2a62eee6-f082-50fe-bdb9-3403ccbb2121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247487} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc0f3c5-1cb0-5ef8-a0d4-769590b54a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247488} {:answer/id #uuid "74174e90-283c-5547-b985-e78264be4d0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc0f3c5-1cb0-5ef8-a0d4-769590b54a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247488} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c1d8ca-5100-5cb9-a5d7-82e3ef59e72e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247484}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "942dc2e8-6f1d-5b4e-8dc9-4a3941397629" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-74439" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/1d687bcdc23c283e0945b039781450d2a020f0fd89c0d2976bb252914b296978.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x & =-4 \\\\ y & =-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x-4$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =x \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-x-4 \\\\ x+x & =-4-2 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-1.$ So, our solution is $x=-3$ and $y=-1.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2)$ is  $(-3,-1).$"}]}
